---
<p>Everyday string work. A <code>string</code> is immutable UTF-8 text: <code>length</code> counts bytes, <code>runeCount</code> characters. Methods that look like edits return a view or a copy; there is no <code>+</code>.</p>

<h2 id="split-join">Split a string and join the parts</h2>
<p><code>parts(by:)</code> walks pieces lazily with <code>for</code>, keeping empty ones; <code>limit:</code> stops early. <code>joined(by:)</code> takes a span of strings, so a list or array goes through <code>.span</code>.</p>
<example name="cookbook-split-join"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">parts = "bilal,maryam,,yusuf".split(",")
" / ".join(["bilal", "maryam", "yusuf"])</code></pre>

<h2 id="format">Format a string and interpolate values</h2>
<p><code>$"…"</code> builds text: any expression goes between the braces, and <code>{{</code> writes a literal brace. Options are named arguments after a comma (<code>as:</code>, <code>width:</code>, <code>fill:</code>, <code>align:</code>, <code>digits:</code>), not a mini-language like <code>{:>6}</code> (rejected).</p>
<example name="cookbook-format"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">f"{n:06} {name:>6} {pi:.2f} {n:x}"</code></pre>

<h2 id="substring">Take a substring and trim whitespace</h2>
<p><code>s[a..b]</code> slices by byte offsets; a slice that cuts a character in half panics. <code>trimmed()</code> drops ASCII whitespace from both ends; <code>from:</code> picks one end, <code>by:</code> trims the runes you list. Results are views, not copies. <code>runes.take(n)</code> counts characters, not bytes.</p>
<example name="cookbook-substring"/>

<h2 id="search-replace">Search, test containment and replace</h2>
<p><code>contains</code>, <code>startsWith</code> and <code>endsWith</code> return <code>bool</code>. <code>position</code> and <code>lastPosition</code> return a byte offset or <code>null</code>: give the missing case <code>??</code> instead of testing <code>-1</code>. <code>replaced(_, by:)</code> returns a copy. <code>equals(_, as: .caseless)</code> compares case-insensitively, folding ASCII letters only, for now.</p>
<example name="cookbook-search"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">i := strings.Index(s, "one") // -1 when missing</code></pre>

<h2 id="regex">Match a regular expression</h2>
<p><code>import std.regex</code>; <code>regex.Regex.parse</code> compiles a pattern and fails <code>RegexError</code> on one it refuses (backreferences, lookaround, named groups). <code>matches</code> tests the whole text; <code>find</code> and <code>findAll</code> return matches, <code>group(0)</code> the matched text. <code>replaced(text, by:)</code> is literal; <code>Regex.escaped</code> builds a pattern from outside input. Matching is linear in the text length.</p>
